.\" mutos_c0.1 - manual page for the MUTOS 1700 cross C compiler front end
.\" Formatted with the standard man(7) macro set (.TH/.SH/.TP/...).
.\" View with: man ./mutos_c0.1   or   groff -man -Tascii mutos_c0.1 | less
.TH MUTOS_C0 1 "September 2026" "MUTOS 1700" "MUTOS 1700 Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
mutos_c0 \- MUTOS 1700 cross C compiler, first pass (front end)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mutos_c0
.I source
.I temp1
.I temp2
.RB [ \-P ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mutos_c0
is the first of the two passes that make up the MUTOS 1700 cross C
compiler (the second is
.BR mutos_c1 (1)).
It lexes and parses
.IR source ,
which is normally the already macro-expanded output of
.BR mutos_cpp (1)
rather than raw C source, and writes a tagged intermediate-code
byte stream to
.I temp1
(with any string-literal text routed through
.I temp2
instead \(en see
.BR OUTPUT ).
.PP
This is a from-scratch, modern-C11 reimplementation of the observable
behavior of the original MUTOS 1700 / Version 7 Unix
.BR cc (1)
compiler's first pass, not a literal port of that pointer-arithmetic
K&R source; see
.I src/mutos_cc/README.md
in the toolchain source tree for the confirmed
.IR temp1 / temp2
wire format (reverse-engineered byte-for-byte from real MUTOS 1700
hardware output) and the exact grammar this pass currently accepts.
.SH "CURRENT SCOPE"
.B mutos_c0
does not yet implement the full K&R C grammar. The exact constructs it
accepts, and the byte-level evidence behind each, are listed in
.I src/mutos_cc/README.md
(\(lqCurrent scope\(rq); in outline:
.IP \(bu 2
function definitions with K&R-style parameter declarations
.RB ( int ,
pointer,
.BR long ,
array and function-pointer parameters);
.IP \(bu 2
local declarations without initializers:
.BR int ,
.BR char ,
.BR long ,
pointers of any degree, one-dimensional arrays, two-dimensional
.B int
arrays, function pointers
.RB ( "int (*f)()" ),
and
.B static
and
.B register
locals; a nested block may declare its own locals, which shadow an
outer declaration of the same name until the block's closing brace;
.IP \(bu 2
file-scope
.BR int ,
.B char
and
.B long
variables without initializers \(en plain (a common block),
.B static
(a block of the file's own) or
.B extern
(a declaration only), a compatible redeclaration included
.RB ( mutos_c1 (1)
does not yet generate code that uses a
.B long
one), and a
.B char
array with a string-literal initializer and no size,
.BR "char text[] = \(dq...\(dq;" ,
plain or
.BR static ;
.IP \(bu 2
assignment statements (\(oq=\(cq and the ten compound-assignment
operators, including subscripted and dereferenced targets),
.BR if / else ,
.BR while ,
.BR do / while ,
.BR for ,
.BR switch / case / default ,
.BR break ,
.BR continue ,
.BR goto /labels,
and
.BR return ;
.IP \(bu 2
expressions over those types: arithmetic, bitwise, shift, relational,
logical and conditional operators, the comma operator, prefix and
postfix
.BR ++ / \-\- ,
unary
.B &
(of a variable or of an array element) and
.BR * ,
array subscripts, string literals, character constants, direct and
indirect function calls,
a narrow set of casts, and
.B sizeof
(folded at compile time);
.IP \(bu 2
.B char
values, with the explicit conversions the MUTOS 1700 front end writes
(opcode 109 in both directions, its type argument the result type): a
.B char
operand of an arithmetic, shift, bitwise, relational or equality
operator is widened to
.BR int ,
and an assignment or
.B return
converts its value to the target's type \(en except that a
.B char
variable, element or dereference compared with a constant from 0 to
127 is not widened: the constant is typed
.B char
instead; where the real compiler converts nothing (a condition, a call
argument, an operand of
.BR && ,
.B ||
or
.BR ! )
a
.B char
variable, element or dereference is passed on unconverted;
.IP \(bu 2
structures and unions (laid out as the real compiler does: members on a
word boundary except
.BR char ,
.B int
and
.B unsigned
bit-fields packed into words, union members at offset 0), members
reached with
.B .
and
.B \->
(written as the real front end retypes them), arrays of structures,
nested structures, assignment of a whole structure of 2 or 4 bytes,
enumerations (their constants plain
.B int
constants),
.B typedef
names, and pointer casts of a call such as
.BR "(struct node *) malloc(n)" .
.PP
A constant subexpression stays folded for as long as every operand
feeding it is constant, matching the real compiler's own front-end
constant folding. Not yet supported, among others: file-scope
pointers, other arrays and other initializers, file-scope structures,
.B static
functions, a function returning a structure or a pointer to one, a
structure passed by value,
.B unsigned
variables, floating point, arrays of three or more dimensions, pointer
subtraction, an ordered comparison of pointers, division, a right shift
or an ordered comparison of an
.B unsigned
value, and a
.B char
value that is not a variable, element or dereference (a cast's, an
assignment's) used as a condition or a call argument.
.PP
Anything outside the supported scope is rejected with a clear
diagnostic and a non-zero exit status, never silently miscompiled.
.SH OUTPUT
.I temp1
receives the bulk of the intermediate code: a sequence of tagged
items, each either a single-byte operator code (immediately followed
by the fixed sentinel byte 0xFE), a little-endian 16-bit data word, or
a NUL-terminated symbol name.
.I temp2
receives only string-literal data: one labelled block of byte values
per literal, then its own terminating marker (a
.B char
array's initializer is written into
.I temp1
instead, unlabelled, where the declaration is). This exactly mirrors the real MUTOS 1700
compiler's own
.IR /lib/c0 ,
which
.BR mutos_cc (1)
(once written \(en see
.BR BUGS )
will invoke in the same way.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I source
Path to the input file (normally
.BR mutos_cpp (1) 's
output). Read directly if given a raw
.B .c
file instead \(en the lexer tolerates comments and whitespace itself
\(en which is convenient for manual testing but not how the real
pipeline invokes this pass.
.TP
.I temp1
Output path for the primary intermediate-code stream.
.TP
.I temp2
Output path for the string-literal side stream.
.TP
.B \-P
Accepted for command-line compatibility with the real MUTOS 1700
.IR /lib/c0 's
profiling-flag position (see
.IR v7/cc/cc.c ).
Not yet acted upon.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors are printed to standard error as
.RI \(dq file : line ": error: " message \(dq
and cause a non-zero exit status; processing continues to the end of
input so that further problems in the same source can be reported in
one pass, matching
.BR mutos_cpp (1) 's
diagnostic convention.
.SH BUGS
.B mutos_cc,
the driver that would normally chain
.BR mutos_cpp (1),
.BR mutos_c0 (1),
.BR mutos_c1 (1),
.BR mutos_as (1),
and
.BR mutos_ld (1)
together into a single pipeline, does not exist yet; invoke this pass
directly (see
.BR SYNOPSIS ),
or see
.I tests/mutos_cc/run_goldens.sh
for a scripted example.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mutos_c1 (1),
.BR mutos_cpp (1),
.BR mutos_as (1)
